## Root cause

In `FilteringParserDelegate.nextToken()`, when `includePath=true` and a single match had just been found and its containing structure closed, the parser used a short-circuit optimization to decide when to stop reading further input (since `allowMultipleMatches=false` means no more matches are needed). That check was:

```java
if (_currToken.isStructEnd()) {
    if (_headContext.isStartHandled()) {
        return (_currToken = null);
    }
}
```

`_headContext.isStartHandled()` is true for *any* ancestor context whose `START_OBJECT`/`START_ARRAY` had already been emitted as part of the included path — not just the root. So as soon as the innermost matched object's closing `END_OBJECT` was returned, the very next `nextToken()` call saw that the parent (path) context was also "start-handled" and terminated immediately, swallowing the closing brackets still owed for the enclosing path (e.g. the final `END_OBJECT` for the outer object).

## Fix

Changed the termination condition to check whether we've actually unwound back to the root context (`_headContext.inRoot()`) rather than merely whether the current context had its start emitted. This lets the parser keep reading and correctly emit the remaining `END_OBJECT`/`END_ARRAY` tokens needed to close out the included path, and only short-circuits once nothing more can possibly be closed (i.e., we're back at the root).

